<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Oxyrhynchus</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Oxyrhynchus, modern el-Bahnasa, was a city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-egypt">Middle Egypt</a> west of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a> and the capital of a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nome">nome</a> in Ptolemaic and Roman times. Excavations by Bernard Grenfell and Arthur Hunt from 1896 recovered hundreds of thousands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papyrus">papyrus</a> fragments from its rubbish mounds. They include tax records, letters and contracts as well as lost works of Greek literature and early Christian texts.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/oxyrhynchus.jpg" alt="Oxyrhynchus" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Abdallah ali · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%D9%85%D9%86%D8%B7%D9%82%D8%A9_%D8%B4%D9%87%D8%AF%D8%A7%D8%A1_%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%A8%D9%87%D9%86%D8%B3%D8%A7.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Oxyrhynchus, also known by its modern name Al-Bahnasa, is a city in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-egypt">Middle Egypt</a> about 160 km south-southwest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a>, west of the main course of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a> on the Bahr Yussef. Its Greek name means sharp-nosed. In ancient Egyptian times a city called Per-Medjed stood on the site, named after the medjed, a species of Nile elephantfish worshipped there as the fish that ate the penis of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osiris">Osiris</a>. It was the capital of the 19th Upper Egyptian nome.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a> conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> in 332 BCE the city was re-established as a Hellenistic town called Oxyrrhynchoupolis. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hellenistic-period">Hellenistic period</a> it was a prosperous regional capital, the third-largest city in Egypt. After Egypt became Christian it was known for its many churches and monasteries. It remained a prominent but gradually declining town in the Roman and Byzantine periods. During the Jewish diaspora uprisings of 115 to 117 CE fighting spread to its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nome">nome</a>, and papyri show that a local festival commemorating the Jewish defeat was still held about eighty years later. A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papyrus">papyrus</a> of 291 CE records an active synagogue.</p><p>Early Islamic chroniclers report that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rashidun-caliphate">Rashidun</a> army under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khalid-ibn-al-walid">Khalid ibn al-Walid</a> took Bahnasa after a battle against a Christian force led by a patrician named Batlus, and then besieged the city; accounts of the siege differ. After the Muslim conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> the canal system on which the town depended fell into disrepair, and Oxyrhynchus was abandoned.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The town of el-Bahnasa occupies part of the ancient site. Because Oxyrhynchus was not considered an Ancient Egyptian site of importance, it was neglected until 1896, when Bernard Pyne Grenfell and Arthur Surridge Hunt of The Queen's College, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxford">Oxford</a>, began to excavate it. Grenfell wrote that the flow of papyri soon became a torrent. Dry conditions had left an unequalled archive of the ancient world, with texts from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ptolemaic-kingdom">Ptolemaic Kingdom</a> and Roman Egypt. Eighty large volumes of the Oxyrhynchus Papyri have been published. Their very ordinariness makes them valuable for social and economic history and for the history of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>. In 2005 a reconstructed poem by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sappho">Sappho</a> was published, part of it first published in 1922 from an Oxyrhynchus papyrus.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Oxyrhynchus” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Tens of thousands of papyri, above all from <b>Oxyrhynchus</b>, the Faiyum villages and Karanis, record leases, taxes, lawsuits, letters and school exercises.</blockquote><a href="/empire/roman-egypt">Roman Egypt · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Much of this literature survives because Byzantine scholars copied it for centuries; papyri found in Egypt since the 19th century, above all at <b>Oxyrhynchus</b>, have restored works thought lost, such as plays of Menander and poems of Sappho.</blockquote><a href="/language/ancient-greek">Ancient Greek · Literature &amp; Learning</a></li><li><blockquote>The oldest extant copies of the Histories are Byzantine manuscripts of the 9th and 10th centuries, notably the Codex Laurentianus A. Many early papyri also survive, including Papyrus <b>Oxyrhynchus</b> 18 and 19 and Papyrus Oxyrhynchus 2099, a fragment of Book VIII from the early 2nd century CE.</blockquote><a href="/work/histories-of-herodotus">Histories of Herodotus · Survival &amp; Rediscovery</a></li><li><blockquote>Papyri such as Papyrus <b>Oxyrhynchus</b> 20 and 21 preserve parts of the text, and in May 2026 a fragment of the Iliad was found placed inside an Egyptian mummy of around 400 CE, the first such text known from a funerary context.</blockquote><a href="/work/iliad">Iliad · Survival &amp; Rediscovery</a></li><li><blockquote>Several <b>Oxyrhynchus</b> Papyri fragments were found to contain parts of the Republic, along with parts of other works such as Phaedo and Gorgias, written around 200 to 300 CE.</blockquote><a href="/work/the-republic">The Republic · Survival &amp; Rediscovery</a></li><li><blockquote>It was found in 1918 at <b>Oxyrhynchus</b> in Egypt and published in 1922, and was kept in the Papyrology Rooms of the Sackler Library in Oxford, renamed the Bodleian Art, Archaeology and Ancient World Library in 2022.</blockquote><a href="/songs/oxyrhynchus-hymn">Oxyrhynchus hymn</a></li></ul></section>
